  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 240 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nSurface analysis early this morning shows strong high pressure in \nplace from SRN WI across Indiana to WVA. GOES19 shows clear skies \nacross Indiana. Aloft water vapor shows high pressure in place over \nthe American SW with ridging extending north, across the high plains \nto MN. This continues to provide subsidence across Indiana. Dew \npoints were in the comfortable 50s.\n\nToday...\n\nRidging aloft to the northwest is expected to push east into the \nGreat Lakes today, keeping Indiana and Ohio under the influence of \nhigh pressure. Forecast soundings today and tonight show a dry \ncolumn as the surface high passes with an inversion aloft. This will \nlead to mostly sunny skies today and mostly clear skies tonight, \nwith temperatures near or slightly below seasonal normals.\n\nWednesday...\n\nA trough within the quick upper flow will be pushing through the \nGreat Lakes on Wednesday, providing the best forcing there. Weaker \nforcing will be found across Indiana. A cold front associated with \nthe trough looks to pass across the area on late Wednesday night. \nForecast soundings through the day show little in the way of lower \nlevel saturation, but do show mid and upper level saturation. \nAfternoon instability appears weak. Thus confidence for any rain on \nWednesday appears low, but better chances will be in play on \nWednesday night with the cold front passage.\n\nThursday...\n\nModels suggest a broad trough in place aloft by Thursday, extending \nfrom Ontario, across Indiana to the Tennessee River Valley. The cold \nfront will have exited to the by Thursday, however forecast \nsoundings suggest diurnal convection will be possible attainable \nconvective temperatures and some CAPE in play. thus a few scattered \nshowers and storms will be possible on Thursday.\n\nFriday through Monday...\n\nSurface high pressure will be building across Indiana in the wake of \nthe previous frontal boundary, leading pleasant weekend weather. The \nupper pattern suggests weak ridging aloft over the high plains and \ninto central Canada, leading to lee side subsidence across the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley. Forecast soundings on Friday through Sunday \nsuggest a very dry column with no CAPE available along and a mid \nlevel inversion in place. Thus dry weather with mostly clear skies \nwill be expected through the weekend. With easterly surface flow on \nFriday slowly moderating to SW flow by Sunday, high temperatures on \nin the lower 80s in Friday to the upper 80s on Sunday.\n\nChance for showers and storms will return on Monday as cold front \napproaches from the northwest. A mid level thermal ridge appears to \nbe in place ahead of the front, pushing toward Indiana, and very \nearly forecast soundings suggest CAPE available in the afternoon \nwith diurnal convection possible. Thus pops at that time look \nreasonable with another day in the upper 80s ahead of the cold \nfront.",
